<problem>
<variantControl numVariants="1" />
<!-- <title>Stability of discrete dynamical systems, x^2</title> -->

<setup>

<function name="f" variables="x">x^2</function>
<function name="tan1" variables="x">0</function>
<function name="tan2" variables="x">2(x-1)+1</function>
<math name="Es">0,1</math>
<math name="Es_unsorted" unordered="true">$Es </math>

</setup>


Consider the dynamical system <m>x_{n+1} = f(x_n)</m>, where <m>f(x)=x^2</m>.


<exercise>
What are the two equilibria of the dynamical system? <m>E=</m> 
<answer>
<award matchPartial="true" symbolicEquality="true">$Es </award>
<award symbolicEquality="true" credit="0.75" name="award1">$Es_unsorted </award>
</answer>
  (Enter in increasing order, separated by commas.)
  
<feedback condition="$award1">Enter your values in increasing order.</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>
What are the slopes of the tangent lines to <m>f(x)</m> at the two equilibria?
</p>
<p>

For the first equilibrium, <m>f'(</m>
  <mathInput name="E1val" />
<m>)\,= </m> 
  <mathInput name="E1slope" />.
</p>

  <answer matchPartial="true" symbolicEquality="true">
    <award referencesAreResponses="$E1val $E1slope">
      <when>$E1val = 0 and $E1slope = 0</when>
    </award>
  </answer>
  
<p>

For the second equilibrium, <m>f'(</m>
    <mathInput name="E2val" />
<m>)\,=</m> 
  <mathInput name="E2slope" />.
</p>

  <answer matchPartial="true" symbolicEquality="true">
    <award referencesAreResponses="$E2val $E2slope">
      <when>$E2val = 1 and $E2slope = 2</when>
    </award>
  </answer>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>
The slopes of the tangent lines, <m>f'(E)</m>, play the role of the slope <m>R</m>, above.  Translate the above conclusions about stability in terms of the slope of the tangent line.
</p>
<p>

An equilibrium <m>E</m> is stable if 
  <mathInput name="stableLower" />
   <m>\lt f'(E) \lt</m> 
  <mathInput name="stableUpper" />
  <answer symbolicEquality="true" matchPartial="true">
    <award referencesAreResponses="$stableLower $stableUpper">
      <when>$stableLower = -1 and $stableUpper = 1</when>
    </award>
  </answer>, which we can rewrite as <m>|f'(E)| \lt</m> 
<answer>
<award symbolicEquality="true">1</award>
</answer>.
</p>
  
<p>

An equilibrium <m>E</m> is unstable if <m>f'(E) \gt</m> 
<answer>
<award symbolicEquality="true">1</award>
</answer>
 or if <m>f'(E) \lt</m> 
<answer>
<award symbolicEquality="true">-1</award>
</answer>
, which we can rewrite as <m>|f'(E)| \gt</m> 
<answer>
<award symbolicEquality="true">1</award>
</answer>
.
</p>
<p>

(We've just stated the 
<ref to="doenet:equilibria_discrete_dynamical_systems_stability">stability theorem</ref>
 for discrete dynamical systems.)
 </p>

<p>Based on the tangent line slopes, determine the stability of the equilibria.
</p>
<p>

The first equilibrium, <m>E=</m> 
<answer>
<award symbolicEquality="true">0</award>
</answer>
 is 
<answer inline="true">
<choiceInput>
<choice credit="1">stable</choice>
<choice>unstable</choice>
</choiceInput>
</answer>
.
</p>
<p>

The second equilibrium, <m>E=</m> 
<answer>
<award symbolicEquality="true">1</award>
</answer>
 is 
<answer inline="true">
<choiceInput>
<choice credit="1">unstable</choice>
<choice>stable</choice>
</choiceInput>
</answer>
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>On the following graph of <m>f(x)=x^2</m> and the diagonal, sketch the tangent lines at the equilibria.  Cobweb to verify your conclusions regarding equilibrium stability. (To verify stability, cobweb with initial conditions near the equilibria.)</p>

  <setup>
    <point name="E1">(0,0)</point>
    <point name="E2">(1,1)</point>
    
    <math extend="$A1.x" name="A1x" />
    <math extend="$A2.x" name="A2x" />
    <derivative name="df">$f</derivative>
    <number name="numPoints">1</number>
    <number name="zeroFixed" fixed="true">0</number>

    <number fixed="true" name="xmin">-2</number>
    <number fixed="true" name="xmax">4</number>
    <number fixed="true" name="ymin">-0.5</number>
    <number fixed="true" name="ymax">3</number>

  </setup>

  <updateValue name="addLine" target="$numPoints" newValue="$numPoints+1" type="number">
    <label>Add line</label>
  </updateValue>
  <updateValue name="deleteLine" target="$numPoints" newValue="max(1,$numPoints-1)" type="number" disabled="$numPoints &lt;= 1">
    <label>Delete line</label>
  </updateValue>

  <graph size="large" aspectRatio="2" identicalAxisScales="true" grid="0.5 0.5" showNavigation="false" xmin="$xmin" xmax="$xmax" ymin="$ymin" ymax="$ymax" name="ex4Graph">
   <xLabel><m>x_n</m></xLabel>
   <yLabel><m>x_{n+1}</m></yLabel>
    <point name="A1" x="-1" y="1" styleNumber="2">
      
        <attractTo>$E1</attractTo>
        <attractTo>$E2</attractTo>
        <constrainTo>$f</constrainTo>
        <constrainToGraph />
      
    </point>
    <point name="A2" x="-1.5" y="2" styleNumber="2">
      
        <attractTo>$E1</attractTo>
        <attractTo>$E2</attractTo>
        <constrainTo>$f</constrainTo>
        <constrainToGraph />
      
    </point>
    <line equation="y=$$df($A1x)(x-$A1x) + $$f($A1x)" fixed="true" styleNumber="2" name="tangentLine1" />
    <line equation="y=$$df($A2x)(x-$A2x) + $$f($A2x)" fixed="true" styleNumber="2" name="tangentLine2" />

<!--     <copy source="f" layer="1" styleNumber="2" hide/> -->
    <cobwebPolyline function="$f" numPoints="$numPoints" name="cobwebPolyline" initialPoint="$initialPoint" lockToSolution="true" draggable="false" />
    <point x="-0.4" y="$initialPoint.x" name="initialPoint" layer="2">
      
        <constrainToGraph />
      
    </point>
  </graph>

  <aside name="calculatedValue">
    <title>Calculated values</title>

    <md>
    <repeat for="$cobwebPolyline.iterateValues" valueName="x" indexName="i">
        <mrow>
          x_{<number>$i-1</number>} \amp = <math displayDigits="5" extend="$x" />
        </mrow>
      </repeat>
    </md>

  </aside>

  <p>Correct tangent lines:
    <answer matchPartial="true">
      <award referencesAreResponses="$A1 $A2">
        <when><mathList unordered="true">$A1 $A2</mathList>
          = <mathList>$E1 $E2</mathList>
        </when>
      </award>
    </answer>
  </p>

  <p>Cobweb at least 3 iterations from just below first equilibrium.
    <answer disableAfterCorrect="true">
      <award referencesAreResponses="$initialPoint $numPoints" credit="$numPoints/6">
        <when>-0.5 &lt; $initialPoint.x &lt;= -0.1</when>
      </award>
      <award credit="0" name="tooCloseBelow">
        <when>-0.1 &lt; $initialPoint.x &lt;= 0</when>
      </award>
    </answer>
  </p>
  <feedback condition="$tooCloseBelow">
    Start at least 0.1 below the equilibrium so we can see what's going on, and make sure to construct 3 complete steps.
  </feedback>


   <p>Cobweb at least 3 iterations from just above first equilibrium.
    <answer disableAfterCorrect="true">
      <award referencesAreResponses="$initialPoint $numPoints" credit="$numPoints/6">
        <when>0.1 &lt;= $initialPoint.x &lt; 0.5</when>
      </award>
      <award credit="0" name="tooCloseAbove">
        <when>0 &lt;= $initialPoint.x &lt; 0.1</when>
      </award>
    </answer>
  </p>
  <feedback condition="$tooCloseAbove">
    Start at least 0.1 above the equilibrium so we can see what's going on, and make sure to construct 3 complete steps.
  </feedback>

  <p>Cobweb at least 6 iterations from just below the second equilibrium.
    <answer disableAfterCorrect="true">
      <award referencesAreResponses="$initialPoint $numPoints" credit="$numPoints/12">
        <when>0.9 &lt;= $initialPoint.x &lt; 1</when>
      </award>
      <award credit="0" name="tooFarBelow">
        <when>0.5 &lt; $initialPoint.x &lt; 0.9</when>
      </award>
    </answer>
  </p>
  <feedback condition="$tooFarBelow">
    Start from within 0.1 of the equilibrium so we can see what's going on, and make sure to construct 6 complete steps.
  </feedback>

    <p>Cobweb at least 4 iterations from just above the second equilibrium.
    <answer disableAfterCorrect="true">
      <award referencesAreResponses="$initialPoint $numPoints" credit="$numPoints/8">
        <when>1 &lt; $initialPoint.x &lt;= 1.1</when>
      </award>
      <award credit="0" name="tooFarAbove">
        <when>1.1 &lt; $initialPoint.x &lt; 1.5</when>
      </award>
    </answer>
  </p>
  <feedback condition="$tooFarAbove">
    Start from within 0.1 of the equilibrium so we can see what's going on, and make sure to construct 4 complete steps.
  </feedback>
</exercise>


<solution>


<exercise>
The equilibria <m>E</m> must satisfy <m>E = E^2</m>.  Subtracting <m>E</m> from both sides, the condition becomes <m>0 = E^2-E = E(E-1)</m>.  The equilibria are <m>E=0</m> and <m>E=1</m>.
</exercise>

<exercise>
<m>f'(x)=2x</m>.  At <m>E=0</m>, the slope is <m>f'(0)=0</m>.  At <m>E=1</m>, the slope is <m>f'(1)=2</m>.
</exercise>

<exercise>
Since <m>f'(0)=0</m> and <m>-1 \lt  0 \lt  1</m>, the equilibrium <m>E=0</m> is stable.  Since <m>f'(1)=2 \gt  1</m>, the equilibrium <m>E=1</m> is unstable.
</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>
A cobwebbing plot from below the first equilibrium:
 <graph size="large" aspectRatio="2" identicalAxisScales="true" grid="0.5 0.5" xmin="$xmin" xmax="$xmax" ymin="$ymin" ymax="$ymax" name="ex4GraphSol1_left" showNavigation="false">
   <xLabel><m>x_n</m></xLabel>
   <yLabel><m>x_{n+1}</m></yLabel>

   <point styleNumber="2" fixed="true" extend="$E1" />
   <point styleNumber="2" fixed="true" extend="$E2" />
    <line equation="y=$$df($E1.x)(x-$E1.x) + $$f($E1.x)" fixed="true" styleNumber="2" name="tangentLine1Sol" />
    <line equation="y=$$df($E2.x)(x-$E2.x) + $$f($E2.x)" fixed="true" styleNumber="2" name="tangentLine2Sol" />

<!--     <copy source="f" layer="1" styleNumber="2" /> -->
    <cobwebPolyline function="$f" numPoints="8" name="cobwebPolylineSol1_left" initialPoint="(-0.4, -0.4)" lockToSolution="true" draggable="false" />
  </graph>  
</p>

<p>
A cobwebbing plot from above the first equilibrium:
 <graph size="large" aspectRatio="2" identicalAxisScales="true" grid="0.5 0.5" xmin="$xmin" xmax="$xmax" ymin="$ymin" ymax="$ymax" name="ex4GraphSol1_right" showNavigation="false">
   <xLabel><m>x_n</m></xLabel>
   <yLabel><m>x_{n+1}</m></yLabel>

    <point styleNumber="2" fixed="true" extend="$E1" />
    <point styleNumber="2" fixed="true" extend="$E2" />
    $tangentLine1Sol
    $tangentLine2Sol

<!--     <copy source="f" layer="1" styleNumber="2" /> -->
    <cobwebPolyline function="$f" numPoints="8" name="cobwebPolylineSol1_right" initialPoint="(0.5, 0.5)" lockToSolution="true" draggable="false" />
  </graph>  
</p>

<p>
A cobwebbing plot from below the second equilibrium:
 <graph size="large" aspectRatio="2" identicalAxisScales="true" grid="0.5 0.5" xmin="$xmin" xmax="$xmax" ymin="$ymin" ymax="$ymax" name="ex4GraphSol2_left" showNavigation="false">
   <xLabel><m>x_n</m></xLabel>
   <yLabel><m>x_{n+1}</m></yLabel>
   
    <point styleNumber="2" fixed="true" extend="$E1" />
    <point styleNumber="2" fixed="true" extend="$E2" />
    $tangentLine1Sol
    $tangentLine2Sol

<!--     <copy source="f" layer="1" styleNumber="2" /> -->
    <cobwebPolyline function="$f" numPoints="12" name="cobwebPolylineSol2_left" initialPoint="(0.95, 0.95)" lockToSolution="true" draggable="false" />
  </graph>  
</p>

<p>
A cobwebbing plot from above the second equilibrium:
 <graph size="large" aspectRatio="2" identicalAxisScales="true" grid="0.5 0.5" xmin="$xmin" xmax="$xmax" ymin="$ymin" ymax="$ymax" name="ex4GraphSol2_right" showNavigation="false">
   <xLabel><m>x_n</m></xLabel>
   <yLabel><m>x_{n+1}</m></yLabel>

    <point styleNumber="2" fixed="true" extend="$E1" />
    <point styleNumber="2" fixed="true" extend="$E2" />
    $tangentLine1Sol
    $tangentLine2Sol

<!--     <copy source="f" layer="1" styleNumber="2" /> -->
    <cobwebPolyline function="$f" numPoints="8" name="cobwebPolylineSol2_right" initialPoint="(1.05, 1.05)" lockToSolution="true" draggable="false" />
  </graph>  
</p>

</exercise>

</solution>


</problem>